/-!
# A fibrewise nonempty free locus after a preliminary blowup

Source: Stacks Project, More on Flatness, Tag 0811, `lemma-flatten-module-pre`,
with Tag 080Y, `remark-successive-blowups`
(blueprint: lem-trf-generic-free-blowup).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules

/-- A finite-type quasi-coherent module on an affine, flat, finitely presented family with
geometrically integral fibres becomes finite locally free on a fibrewise nonempty open after
an admissible blowup. The centre is retained by `IsAdmissible` and `moduleStrictTransform`.
Finite locally free allows the finite rank to vary between trivializing opens. Relative
flatness at a generic point means flatness of the original module stalk over the local ring
of the base, with scalars restricted along the canonical stalk map. Finite presentation of
`f` needs no extra quasi-compactness or quasi-separatedness binders: an affine morphism has
both properties.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0811, `lemma-flatten-module-pre`
(blueprint: lem-trf-generic-free-blowup).

Proof: First use `lem-trf-module-admissible-blowup-calculus` to compose successive admissible
blowups and identify successive strict transforms. It also preserves finite type and
quasi-coherence. Flatness and finite presentation survive base change by
`lem-weil-pbc-flat-morphism-base-change` and
`lem-weil-pbc-finite-presentation-base-change-composition`; fibres after base change are
field extensions of the original fibres and remain geometrically integral.

For each point of U, the local ring of the integral fibre at its generic point is a field.
Relative flatness and finite presentation therefore give a free neighbourhood by
`lem-trf-flat-fibre-free-neighbourhood`. Its image is open by `lem-weil-pbc-flat-lfp-open`.
Every nonempty open of an integral fibre contains its generic point, so generic rank is
locally constant on U. Quasi-compactness gives finitely many quasi-compact clopen rank strata.

Choose a finite-type boundary ideal by `lemma-nagata-qc-open-complement-ideal-exists` and
principalize it using `det-nagata-boundary-divisor-cartier`. The preserved open is now
scheme-theoretically dense. Separate the closures of the rank strata by
`def-nagata-separate-constructible-closed` and `lemma-nagata-separate-constructible-closed`.
Density, preserved by `lemma-nagata-admissible-blowup-preserves-density`, makes these closures
cover the base, hence makes them clopen. For empty U density makes the new base empty.

Cover the rank pieces by finitely many affine opens where the boundary is generated by a
nonzerodivisor, using `lemma-nagata-invertible-ideal-is-effective-cartier` and
`def-nagata-effective-cartier-ideal`. On each chart the family and module correspond to a
flat finitely presented algebra and a finite module whose boundary localization is finitely
presented. Use `lem-weil-pbc-lfp-affine-opens-finite-presentation`,
`lem-weil-pbc-quasi-coherent-finite-type-affine`, and the affine-section characterization in
`def-pc-scheme-module-flat-over` to verify these hypotheses.

Apply `lem-trf-affine-generic-rank-blowup` on every chart. The blowup charts cover by
`prop-nagata-affine-blowup-chart-cover`. On a chart the strict transform is the quotient by
power torsion of the exceptional generator: sections vanishing away from the exceptional
set are exactly those killed by a power, by `def-weil-pbc-module-localization`. The helper's
free principal opens meet every fibre; their union gives the required local good open.

Extend each chart blowup by `def-nagata-extended-admissible-blowup` and
`lemma-nagata-extend-admissible-blowup`. A finite common refinement is obtained by successively
multiplying centres, using `def-nagata-blowup-product-comparison` and
`thm-nagata-blowup-product-ideal`; the comparison maps are composites of admissible blowups.
Their restrictions to opens remain blowups by `def-nagata-blowup-flat-base-change-map` and
`thm-nagata-blowup-flat-base-change`. On each good open a finite free module over the flat
family is flat over the base. The calculus thus identifies its next strict transform with
ordinary pullback, preserving the free trivializations. The good open remains surjective
by `lem-weil-pbc-surjective-base-change`. Unite these pulled-back opens and compose all
preliminary blowups by the calculus to obtain a single blowup of the original base. -/
theorem exists_admissibleBlowup_fiberwise_finiteLocallyFree
    {X S : Scheme.{u}} [CompactSpace S] [QuasiSeparatedSpace S]
    (f : X ⟶ S) [IsAffineHom f] [Flat f] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation f]
    (F : X.Modules) [F.IsQuasicoherent] [F.IsFiniteType]
    (U : S.Opens) (hU : IsCompact (U : Set S))
    (hfp : (F.restrict (f ⁻¹ᵁ U).ι).IsFinitePresentation)
    (hgeom : ∀ s : S, GeometricallyIntegral (f.fiberToSpecResidueField s))
    (hflat : ∀ (s : S), s ∈ U → ∀ (ξ : f.fiber s), IsGenericPoint ξ Set.univ →
      @Module.Flat (S.presheaf.stalk (f (f.fiberι s ξ)))
        (F.presheaf.stalk (f.fiberι s ξ)) _ _
        (Module.compHom _ (f.stalkMap (f.fiberι s ξ)).hom)) :
    ∃ B : IsAdmissible S U, ∃ V : (Limits.pullback f B.I.blowupMap).Opens,
      Surjective (V.ι ≫ pullback.snd f B.I.blowupMap) ∧
      SheafOfModules.IsFiniteLocallyFree
        ((@moduleStrictTransform X S f B.I
          (B.I.isFiniteType_toModules_sheaf B.finiteType) F inferInstance).restrict V.ι) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules
